      "content": "Scholarship generally views Alcibiades through a nuanced lens that acknowledges his strategic opportunism without automatically labeling him a moral monstrosity. His fluctuating allegiances—shifting between Athens, Sparta, and Persia—were largely calculated moves to maximize his own influence and justify his influence on contemporary observers. Ultimately, history suggests he was a formidable real political player who traded ethics for power rather than a simple idealist or heartless traitor.",
